r hit  squad  is trans and just is  never comes up never impacts the story  and here's the thing this  kind of comes into this whole thing  where so often  people feel that we need to be justified  if we're going to be there which i've  talked about before in that video  but just to reiterate briefly it is so  frequent  for anyone who deviates from the  template of straight  white heterosexual cisgendered  and able-bodied if you deviate from any  and definitely if you deviate from all  five there has to be a reason you can't  just be there  something about the story must  necessitate you being there otherwise  what are you doing here  i know joss whedon has become a bit of a  difficult figure to talk about in some  ways but i'm still going to pull this  quote from him because it  is a great answer now granted he was  talking about female characters as  opposed to this but the principle's the  same he got asked in an interview  why do you write so many strong female  characters and his answer was  i write them because you have to keep  asking that question  that's the thing as long as  mainstream and a significant number of  people are saying but why  are they there then clearly there isn't  enough representation  we need to show up often enough  frequently enough  in enough things in enough numbers not  tokenly but meaningfully that eventually  people stop asking why we're there  and eventually maybe it can get to the  point we're saying  why is why is there a non-binary  character in this story would be like  asking  why is there a left-handed person in  this story  you wouldn't ask that that's a  ridiculous question why wouldn't there  be a left-handed person what  that that's the goal  that's what we want we don't  want to be treated special we actually  want to be treated like everybody else  and also at times ignored like everybody  else  but a lot of us can't be  and often by choice in terms of our  presentation  but look what i do in terms of sitting  down in